  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Monday)\nIssued at 105 AM CDT TUE JUL 21 2026\n\nGuidance and NHC tract continue to keep Tropical Storm Bertha\nmoving a little further to the northwest before turning to the\nwest and hugging the Louisiana Gulf Coast Wednesday and Thursday\nbefore the most likely outcome of a landfall along the Southeast\nTexas Coast late Friday. Impacts from Betha's northwest then west\ntrack for Central Alabama will be east to southeast winds slightly\nelevated with a few minor gusts, but under wind advisory for \nTuesday and a few showers across mainly the southeast half of the \narea. At the same time upper high pressure will be situated from \nWest Texas to Oklahoma and Arkansas. Northwest upper flow will \nresult across the Dakotas and Upper Plains from being wedged \nbetween the ridge and an Eastern Canada low. Also over Eastern \nCanada will be a surface low that will drag a front across the \nGreat Lakes and Mid Mississippi River Valley through mid week with\nfavorable assistance from the upper northwest flow. However, this\nfront is expected to stall across the middle part of Alabama late\nWednesday into Thursday. It is expected to remain across Central \nAlabama through late Friday into early Saturday before pushing \nback northward into Tennessee as a warm front. This will keep the \narea in a wet pattern more so after Bertha departs from mid week \ninto this weekend.\n\nWhile most of the convection from Bertha is along the southeast\nsections, hot and humid air will continue to allow for heat\nindices to approach up to the 105-106 range for the northwest\nsections for Tuesday once again. A heat advisory remains in\neffect. These conditions may linger for one more day for\nWednesday depending on the speed of the system/front coming from\nthe northwest. Later today if guidance continues to indicate\nthis, the heat advisory may need to be extended for one more day\nfor Wednesday.\n\n08",
